talk: Greg Fahy presenting some data from the TRIIM and TRIIM-X trials

video: <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wknFx0zhQcA>

speaker: Greg Fahy

date: 2026-05-25

LLM-generated summary: The presentation argues that progressive thymic involution is a central driver of immunosenescence and late-life mortality, with quantitative data showing 75-90% loss of functional thymic mass by the early thirties, followed by exponential contraction of the naive T-cell receptor repertoire (the Naylor Cliff) between ages 62-78. Regeneration protocols combining growth hormone, DHEA, metformin, and zinc restored thymic density on MRI, increased naive CD4/CD8 counts and recent thymic emigrants by amounts equivalent to 11-28 years of immunologic rejuvenation, lowered CRP by ~50%, reversed multiple epigenetic clocks by 1-2.5 years, and produced functional gains in strength, VO2 max, and cardiovascular parameters with an acceptable safety profile. The speaker further contends that transgenic growth-hormone/IGF-1 longevity models are developmental artifacts rather than evidence of pro-aging effects, and outlines an upcoming "immortal thymus" transgenic mouse study.

description: In this Conference talk, Dr. Greg Fahy presents stunning data from the TRIIM and TRIIM-X trials. His team has successfully regrown the human thymus in older adults, reversed epigenetic aging clocks by up to two years, and restored immune function to levels seen decades earlier. Beyond the lab results, participants showed dramatic real-world improvements: 15% stronger muscles, 21% better VO2 max, and frailty scores dropping to near zero. Dr. Fahy also unveils the "Immortal Thymus Project"—a plan to prove that maintaining a youthful thymus could double lifespan. This is one of the most promising rejuvenation protocols in development today.

[[!toc levels=3]]

# Thymic Involution and Mortality Risk

By the time you're 25 or so, you've lost 75% of your functional thymic mass. This is kind of important because, as most of you will know, you need the thymus to make T-cells, which is a cornerstone of your immune system. By the time you're 31 or so, you've lost 90% of your functional thymic mass. But why do we survive? We survive because the cells that you made in youth last for a really long time, but they run out eventually. So my thesis is that most people die of an indirect consequence of thymic involution. So we can study aging and its facets, but what do we actually die of? I would contend that most of us die indirectly of thymic involution.

# Surrogate Markers from Infectious Disease and Cancer

So the evidence kind of starts with the death rates from the flu. You can see on the leftmost panel your risk of dying from the flu goes up by a factor of a hundred logarithmically as you go from the age of 10 to about the age of 70 and then a little bit later in life we have data for COVID-19 again the same hundred fold increase in your risk of death as you go from a little older than 20 years of age and then a little bit later than that prostate cancer comes in. So I look at infectious disease death risk and cancer death risk as surrogate markers for thymic failure because the thymus can clean up infectious diseases, it can clean up cancer. So if you die of these things it's a sign that your thymus is not really working as well as it should. So if you add up all of the deaths from infectious disease and cancer and you plot that as a function of total death risk, by the age of 50, about 30% of your total risk of death is from causes that relate back to the functional integrity of your thymus. And you can just imagine where that curve extrapolates to after the age of 50, but the CDC didn't have the data when I compiled this figure.

# The Naylor Cliff and Immune Repertoire Collapse

But to get an inkling of it, I'm going to introduce you to something called the Naylor Cliff. That's what I call it. So that's a sum total of all the antigens that your immune system can recognize as foreign. So these are anything abnormal that might want to kill you or end up killing you. So you actually do pretty well until the age of 62. You are like a 20 year old until the age of 62 but between 62 and 78 you lose two orders of magnitude from your immune repertoire the versatility of your immune system so considering what happened to you before the age of 50 you can only imagine what happens between the beginning and end of the Naylor Cliff so I would contend that that actually pushes your risk of death from thymus related causes over 50% and there's much more evidence in support of that.

# Mechanistic Links to Cellular Senescence and Organ Aging

For example for those are interested in telomere biology and cellular senescence the functional immune system can remove senescent cells but it was recently learned that an activated CD4 T cell can actually restore telomeres to cells that are deficient in telomeres transfers them bodily from the CD4 cell into the cells that are senescent reverses senescence cleans up senescent cells in organs like the brain the liver the kidney etc but beyond that if you give a thymus transplant to an old mouse it reverses aspects of brain aging and liver aging and we're working with a group at Wake Forest that believes that the thymus regulates vascular health as well and of course vascular health underlies cardiovascular disease which kills most of us.

# Evidence from Human Thymectomy

So just to convince you a little bit more before we dig into the details a study was published in New England Journal of Medicine a couple years ago about people who had open heart surgery and of course the thymus is right under your breastbone so when they go through your sternum to get to the heart half the time they throw your thymus away. It's kind of a nuisance. So somebody got the bright idea of going back and seeing what happens to people that have that happen. Bear in mind this is people who are already late in life, they've already lost most of their thymic mass. This data shows that you need every shred of thymus you can get because if they take your thymus out compared to not taking it out, your total risk of death within five years goes up by a factor of three. That's death from all causes. Risk of death from cancer almost two and a half times. Risk of cancer occurrence doubles. The risk of cancer recurrence goes up by fourfold. And if you do have your cancer recur after you've had a thymectomy, the chances that it comes back in severe, hard to control form go up by a factor of seven.

# Recent Human Observational Data on Thymic Function

And then last month in Science a couple of papers came out I just extracted some of the data. If you have high thymic function versus low thymic function you obviously have a much higher potential to survive. But of course this paper looks at thymic function by the metric of older people. And this is just a ghost of what you could achieve if you could actually have a younger thymus. In the bottom panel there on the right, it just shows that cardiovascular deaths are dramatically higher in people that have poorly functioning thymuses compared to ones that have good thymuses. So I think the thymus is really, really important.

# Therapeutic Approach to Thymus Regeneration

So what do you do about it? Well what we do about it is we regrow the thymus. So it's been known in many animal models and in human models, HIV patients, that you can regrow the human thymus with growth hormone. But growth hormone has side effects. We combine growth hormone with DHEA and metformin to get rid of the diabetogenic effect of growth hormone and zinc to prevent nutritional deficiencies so what we showed in our paper in Aging Cell in 2019 is that we can regrow the thymus in people over the age of 50 so our trial was 50 to 65 year olds.

# MRI and Imaging Evidence of Regeneration

And you see in the upper left pair of panels there that the thymus looks darker in an MRI scan that's because fat looks whiter or paler gray and you can see the darkening so below that you see some three-dimensional renderings of the thymus is sort of subdivided it into areas that were highly active and highly fatty so fat is yellow and white and highly active is red and you can see that the more functional part of the thymus is practically non-existent before we regenerated it but very extensive afterwards. Can it be regenerated I mean can this be duplicated the answer is yes. So in TRIM so we've done a series of studies TRIM X A B C and a few others so but in TRIM X B we repeated these MRI measurements the color coding is different but the result is the same you see that in the bottom panel after regeneration we have a lot of blue and green which is coded to be functional thymic mass.

# Age-Independence of Regeneration Capacity

Alright, so the question really is, okay, we can regenerate the thymus, but does it do you any good? Well actually before we even get to that can we regenerate thymus independent of your age. The answer to that seems to be yes at least so far so in the upper right pair of panels I expressed the thymus result that we documented in terms of the increment in the percent of the thymus that's functional and I did that because of what you see on the left so that's that same figure I showed you before percent of peak functional mass versus age. By the time you're between 40 and 60 you're down around 5% of the amount of functional mass you had before so if we could increment that by even 5% we would take you back in immunological time quite substantially so what you see on the upper right is the raw data for individual thymic increments over time in our original study in 2019 and then just those results plotted against age and you see that our R squared shows there's zero correlation between age and functional ability to regenerate the thymus and then in TRIM X B we show the same results there's one guy who didn't regenerate his thymus very well but he was right in the middle of the age range so once again there's no age dependence so we can do this people of any age so far up to the age of 68 that's as high as we've looked so far.

# Restoration of Naive T-Cell Populations and RTEs

So does this do you any good does the thymus actually do anything so what the thymus is supposed to do is to produce new CD4 and CD8 T cells. So we looked at three different flavors of these, just naive CD8s and naive CD4s, and then a very, very freshly shed CD4 type naive T cell called a recent thymic emigrant. And so what you see is in our original paper and then in our follow-up studies, the naive CD8 T cells increased by 20%. That may not seem like a lot but that's sufficient to negate 11 years of previous losses of those cells due to aging so actually that's like the equivalent of 11 years of aging reversal in that compartment and same with CD4 T cells we only increase them by 13 percent but actually that's the equivalent of about 28 years of losses of those cells and actually that's really underestimating the effect because that ignores the effect of the Naylor Cliff on the versatility of those cells that are coming back. All the new cells have new receptors that you're adding to your stock.

# Impact on Recent Thymic Emigrants and Repertoire Rescue

So the middle panel there shows the recent thymic emigrant studies that we've done. I've only got two examples. It can get kind of busy, but this is a little difficult because the RTEs decline with age on an exponential scale, not a linear scale. But I've given you two examples. So we had a guy who was 71 years old. We took him back to an RTE age about 15 years younger. And the point I want to make about him is that if you compare him to the Naylor Cliff which is reproduced above that he's that red vertical line so he was well down that Naylor Cliff and we dragged him back up that cliff and put him up on that plateau now at least superficially you know there's a lot more analysis that has to be done but that's very encouraging and then the female volunteer she was right before falling off the Naylor Cliff and we dragged her back from that so that means she had a really good repertoire but now she's not going to lose it a few years later she's going to keep that repertoire for a long long time so we're really excited about that.

# Reduction in Exhausted T Cells and Functional Improvements

And then the other thing that happens when you expand the number of new T cells in the immune system is the old T cells have to make way because your immune space is limited so as new T cells enter old cells have to be gotten rid of so we saw a reduction in exhausted PD-1 positive CD8 T cells which block your ability to reject cancer. So Opdivo and Keytruda rake in billions to do the same thing, but we have no side effects and it's cheap and it lasts a long time. And that's just by making your immune system younger again, it's just a natural effect of that.

# T-Cell Functional Stimulation Index Recovery

So we wanted to now go to the next step beyond that and say, okay, we got new T cells, that's great, but do the new T cells actually do anything? Do they have improved function? So we just started off recently looking at the stimulation index. So this is on our latest trial, the TRIM-XP trial. We'll bring up a little bit more about that shortly. But you see that baseline 25 to 30 year old T cell stimulation index around 4. You get up to 45, it's down around 2 and a half. So in our cohort in XP, we had people with median age of 62 and their baseline T cell stimulation was similar to the 45 year olds, but within three months you could begin to see the curve go up and at six months we had statistically significant increases in stimulation index similar to what you see in youth so that very encouraging.

# Anecdotal Reversal of Immune Risk Phenotype

And then this one case you know it just one case so it's an anecdote but it's so amazing I just had to share it with you so one of the guys the oldest guy in the XP trial he entered we didn't know it at the time but he entered with an inverted CD4 CD8 T-cell ratio that's a signature of what has been called the immune risk phenotype this happens if you have viral infection turns out he has EBV so EBV CMV viruses like that they re-present themselves the immune system over and over and over again and your immune system does what it should it raises up T cells to get rid of them CD8 T cells to get rid of them but that drives out your naive T cells so over time your immune system gets weaker and weaker you're really good at getting rid of EBV but you don't need that you're already able to do that so what I've never seen is a reversal of the inversion so after 120 days on our treatment his ratio went almost up to normal so we're really hoping we can reproduce that.

# Effects on Systemic Inflammation

Okay so the other aspect of aging and immunity is inflammation and there's plenty of ways of measuring inflammation and we have to plead poverty we couldn't afford most of these assays but we did do CRP and it's a really good general purpose measure of the general inflammatory state of the person. In our original TRIM study, we found a reduction of about 50% after nine months and pretty much the same result after 12 months. In TRIM XA, we measured this again in men and women. We actually had six women in the trial. We don't have complete data for this in more than three of them. You see, if anything, an even bigger drop in CRP and the result is highly statistically significant even when you combine the two sexes.

# Epigenetic Age Reversal and Validation

Okay, so then the reason that our paper really caught a lot of attention is it showed something completely new at the time. So as you all know, if you enter a clinical trial and it lasts for one year, normally at the end of that one year, you would be a year older biologically. Now if you could achieve Aubrey's concept of longevity escape velocity or at least get right up to the edge of it, then you would escape aging. So for every year that went by, you reverse your aging by one year you don't age at all, right? That's that horizontal dotted line. What we showed is that at the end of one year in the trial instead of being one year older you were one and a half years younger which means that we reverse the year that you on the trial plus one and a half years on top of that. Now Irena Conboy pointed out you know these clocks don't have mechanistic significance in most cases so does this really mean anything and I think that it really does because regardless of mechanisms these clocks do predict what's going to happen to you.

# Mortality Prediction by GrimAge and Vascular Links

And so this is a paper that showed that in a hundred and one thousand ninety one Scots living in Edinburgh who were followed until they were 70 years of age or so if you measure their GrimAge at baseline and then followed them for mortality afterwards you could predict which ones were going to die and which ones weren't with pretty good accuracy based on their GrimAge so if you had a low GrimAge not very old by GrimAge standards your mortality was way way lower than if you had a high GrimAge and also your brain worked and you didn't have as many vascular lesions either getting back to the potential connection between what I would say the thymus and the vascular system but in any case so that's what we showed in TRIM so the question then is can we reproduce that and the answer to that is yes too.

# Reproducibility Across Multiple Epigenetic Clocks

So on the left you see in the upper panel the average of four different epigenetic aging clocks going back about two and a half years at nine at 12 months and then below that Steve Horvath went back and looked at our plasma data and applied it to a completely different non-epigenetic clock the Morgan Levine plasma PhenoAge clock and found that he saw the same effect so in TRIM XA we reproduce the four clocks that went back in reverse and we added four more clocks to that four clock categories 21 clocks in all and these are clocks that predict whether you're going to have chronic conditions, functional impairment, cognitive dysfunction, or death within the next three to five years.

# Accelerated Reversal in TRIM-XP and Long-Term Stability

In XP, we decided to see if we could accelerate these aging reversal effects, and the answer is yes. So you see in the upper curve there on the right that we didn't get much aging reversal in TRIM XA but in TRIM XP we got age down by one to two years in just one to two months it usually takes nine to twelve months for that. I'm just going to make this point because going back to that longevity escape velocity concept we had two guys that entered the TRIM trial and then entered the XA trial later on and this is what their Levine ages look like over time and as you can see if you take the average of them out to eight or nine years we're not finding any Levine age changes so that's kind of interesting I'm making no claims about this it's just too interesting though to hold on to I wanted to share that.

# Ancillary Observations and Physiological Gains

More evidence as we saw hair darkening in TRIM and in our follow-up studies although in different ways eyebrows eyelashes going from white to dark again hair quality at least coming back so far early in TRIM XP. Okay so more physiological evidence that these aging clocks are actually measuring something real what do they do for you in the real world well they make you stronger so this is like the six million dollar man I call it the six million dollar thymus you know we can make you stronger faster better well we can make you stronger apparently so without any exercise people in our trial showed a 15% increase in muscle strength which is the equivalent to 4 to 11 years of previous decline in muscle strength depending on how fast your decline would have gone as baseline people vary in that regard. Now there's one guy that just blows everything off so you're I consider him to be like the incredible Hulk this guy could do 120 push-ups at baseline and after nine months he could do 50% more than that after 15 months he doubled it so your mileage may vary I can't claim everyone will have these results but it's awfully neat. We improve VO2 max by 21% that's equivalent to 22 years of aging reversal in VO2 max which is highly related to mortality if we take this at face value that would predict a reduction in mortality 40% over 11 years. In TRIM XP after three months on the trial a guy who was taking 12 minutes to run a mile could start running the mile in eight minutes and we had a guy in XA who started doing 5K races at the age of 81 and started winning them and we've had other anecdotal reports along the same lines. Frailty went almost to zero this is a sit-stand test going to up with immense statistical significance and then these radical improvements in cardiovascular risk factors dropping your diastolic blood pressure from 85 to 65 dropping your resting pulse rate from 72 to 62 these are significant and we've had a lot of anecdotal reports of other things just going right on the trial.

# Body Composition and Safety Profile

You also get skinnier people lost four kilograms of fat and they gained about 1.3 kilograms of lean body mass. We think the trial is safe. PSA went down in original study didn't go down in XA but it didn't go up either. Percent free PSA went up which means less cancer risk. We have sometimes an inability to completely control the diabetogenic effect of growth hormone so sometimes insulin goes up sometimes it doesn't but glucose and hemoglobin A1c actually go down although not significantly and then creatinine change very very slightly.

# Addressing Myths Around Growth Hormone and IGF-1

So I want to just touch on the end here about this further safety so there's a lot of mythology in aging biology that growth hormone and IGF-1 are pro-aging factors this is not true and one of the pieces of evidence is that to this is that you would expect that if you gave massive doses of growth hormone to a mature animal not a transgenic animal whose developmental program was changed by the transgenic modification you expect that they would live a shorter life. That not the case so here studies in male and female rats and mice given massive human doses of growth hormone into a mouse or to a rat for their whole life or for the later part of it in no case showing life shortening and Arthur Everitt did a tumorigenesis study and found no increase in tumors in animals that were getting up to well 4,000 times the human dose of growth hormone. The FDA does not recognize cancer as a side effect of growth hormone and there have been other studies that say the same thing.

# Future Directions and Closing

We're gearing up to go after the finals for the hundred one million dollar XPRIZE Healthspan competition which we've got some pretty good data thank you we're we haven't been approved yet but our fingers crossed we've got good immune data and muscle data that I've told you about before their third criteria is we have to reverse brain aging also turns out there's all kinds of reasons I think that we might do that we have some very early data showing we can measure brain aging reversal of about 4.6 years 3.2 years etc including increased white matter content of your brain which tends to go down as you get older and positive trends in gray matter so we have other stuff on the drawing board but I'm out of time so I'm not going to go into that except I just want to touch on the immortal thymus project. If the thymus is really as important as I'm making it out to be here if you never had to undergo thymic involution what would that do to your lifespan well nobody knows but we can find out so there is a transgenic mouse model that can maintain his thymus forever and you can start it before the thymus ever involutes so this is going to be the immortal thymus project we think it double the lifespan of mice it never been done before so come to our website and maybe give us some contributions for this because we need them so I just want to thank you on behalf of myself and my team at Intervene Immune and all of the volunteers who have worked very hard to make it possible for me to give this presentation to you today including Walter Crompton who's in the audience right there. All right, Walter so if I lied to you I'm sure Walter will tell you all about it so thanks for your attention that's it.

# Q&A

**Q:** Greg you and I we spoke about the work that was done in mice transgenic mice and I think I don't need to tell you about it but could you please refer to that or talk about that briefly? And the growth hormone.

**A:** So what Steve is referring to is a little story that I told him about a paper that none of you have read, but you should all read if you're in the biology of aging and you think that IGF-1 is important and growth hormone is important for aging or a driver of aging. So it turns out that these transgenic animal models are an artifact. So what actually happens is that in the normal course of brain aging, in the normal course of your life, if you're a mouse, you finish wiring your hypothalamus around, I forget the exact day, but around day five of postnatal development. And what that does, as Dongsheng Cai's lab has pointed out, is it causes an inflammatory state to develop in the medial basal hypothalamus in which the neural stem cells in that area start spewing stuff into your cerebrospinal fluid that goes into your body that programs body aging. And the reason that these transgenic animal models work is because they block the rewiring of the brain that takes place in day five of postnatal development. And it has nothing to do with what happens with aging other than that. And the reason that they were able to prove that is if you give growth hormone on day five of postnatal development, the transgenic animals don't live longer than control animals, even though they have the transgene even though they don't express growth hormone and IGF-1. So it's all been misdirection. So I've suspected this for a long time but anyway that paper puts the frosting on the cake. So I don't think that IGF-1 and growth hormone are pro-aging. Now they can be. I mean there are side effects. Don't get me wrong. You've got to be careful about this but it's not like people think and I think that actually people who are focusing on getting rid of IGF-1 as a remedy for aging are dooming the recipients of that treatment to death because you need IGF-1. You know, if you give growth hormone to an old person it regenerates their kidneys, it regenerates their hearts, it regenerates a lot of stuff and people don't know about that either but you know you're losing cells you need cells you have to have aging is catabolic and growth hormone makes things grow it's anabolic so as long as you can deal with the cancer issue which maybe we can deal with pretty well if you have a really great immune system, it's got to be part of an anti-aging intervention in my opinion. That's my opinion.

# Insights

- Thymic output directly gates both the quantity and diversity of naive T cells; once diversity collapses past the Naylor Cliff threshold, infection and cancer mortality rise sharply.  
- Activated CD4 cells can donate telomeres to senescent somatic cells, providing a direct mechanistic bridge from thymic function to clearance of senescence.  
- Thymic regeneration expands new T-cell clones while simultaneously displacing exhausted PD-1+ CD8 cells, yielding an endogenous checkpoint-inhibitor-like effect.  
- Epigenetic clocks and plasma PhenoAge both move in the same direction, implying that thymic rejuvenation produces system-wide physiologic reversal measurable by orthogonal biomarkers.

